<figure><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-400.webp 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-800.webp 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-3000.webp 3000w" sizes="auto"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-400.jpeg" alt="A collection of stickers on the dark grey lid of a ThinkPad laptop. There's a NixOS logo in the centre, a cartoon pigeon looking at a 'nest consisting of two sticks and and egg and saying 'Meh. Good enough', and other nerd-adjacent logos, anti-AI stickers and so on." width="3000" height="2250" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-400.jpeg 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-800.jpeg 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/tSuCuFoj-4-3000.jpeg 3000w" sizes="auto"></picture><figcaption>Stickers on the lid so far. I’m leaving room to add more.</figcaption></figure><p>Since <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/all-aboard-the-nixos-train-again/">installing NixOS</a> on a little Minisforum PC, I’ve been a full-time NixOS user at home (I use a MacBook Pro at work). I’m going to be finishing work next Autumn, so I will probably put some form of Linux on the MacBook at that point. However, as it is an Apple Silicon machine and it has a T-whatever-it-is security chip in it, my options may be practically limited to installing <a href="https://asahilinux.org">Asahi Linux</a> rather than NixOS. I may also dual-boot with macOS for a while to keep a foot in that camp and maintain compatibility with iOS, if I am still using an iPhone then. Asahi is a heroic project, given the hurdles of trying to reverse engineer some very proprietary and locked-down hardware, but there is a limit to what they can do in terms of compatibility. I decided to buy a used PC laptop to install NixOS on, and settled on a Lenovo ThinkPad X1 Carbon (Gen 9). I’ll explain why I chose this model, how the installation went, and the upgrades I made.</p><h3>Why a ThinkPad?</h3><p>The Lenovo ThinkPads generally have a good reputation for compatibility with Linux, and because they are very common, there’s good information out there, including <a href="https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Laptop/Lenovo">exhaustive compatibility documentation on the ArchLinux wiki</a>, which is very handy to compare models or generations. The ThinkPads are also generally robust and capable workhorses, as befits a machine which is bought in huge quantities as the laptop of choice for the corporate workforce. The latter point is also a big advantage if you are buying used, as it means that there’s a plentiful supply of units on the market (refurbished or not) that can be bought relatively cheaply. But which one should I go for?</p><p>Friends, there are <em>so many models</em> of ThinkPad. What I wanted was a fairly small and light machine (about a 13 or 14 inch screen), which steered me towards the X1 Carbon range. I also wanted a model which had USB-C/Thunderbolt connections, because I needed to connect to my external monitor and existing USB-C peripherals. Of course I could get some kind of hub or dongle, but my aim was to get the heck out of Dongle Town after living there for a bit with the Mac ecosystem a few years ago. Finally, my only other item on the wishlist was a high resolution screen. I’ve been thoroughly spoiled by 4K screens, but my eyesight is also not what it was (I have early-onset cataracts) and I find lower resolution screens extremely tiring now.</p><p>I haven’t investigated the other ThinkPad models extensively, but many generations of the X1 Carbon models seem to have user-replaceable batteries and SSD hard drives, but not RAM as that is generally soldered in. I found that so refreshing after ‘nothing is user serviceable’ Mac hardware landscape. It also helped me narrow down my search further. I could make sure that any model I was considering still had replacement batteries available for sale, and I could prioritise units with decent amount of RAM, but not worry too much about SSD capacity. Digging around, I found that the Generation 9 and 10 units of the X1 Carbon had the ports and high res screens (in at least some variants), and you can readily get hold of batteries. If you want to save some money and are less concerned about USB-C, then Generations 2 to 7 also seem pretty well supported, but I had trouble finding batteries for Gen 8 (for some reason), so I would avoid that one.</p><p>Equipped with these constraints to narrow my search, I headed to eBay. Again, there are <em>so many</em> ThinkPads on eBay. I got a bit lucky as I found a Gen 9 unit (from 2021 or 2022) at a great price, which had 32GB of RAM, a 500GB SSD and a 4K screen. It was from a refurb company, so there was also the peace of mind of a one year guarantee. When it arrived, I was pleasantly surprised that it seemed much less beaten up physically than I would have expected from a ‘Grade C’ unit.</p><h3>Installing NixOS</h3><p>As I have written about before, <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/using-denix-for-a-multi-host-nix-config/">I use Denix</a> to help structure my NixOS configuration for multiple hosts, but this was actually the first test of the multi-host system! In practice, it was all very easy: I added a new directory in <code>/hosts</code> in <a href="https://codeberg.org/bsag/nixos">my configuration</a> to define the features I wanted on the new machine. I then used the NixOS installer (the graphical one, because I like an easy life) to install a basic installation. This gave me <code>configuration.nix</code> and <code>hardware.nix</code> files tailored to the new machine, which I could then integrate into the <code>/host/bright/default.nix</code> and <code>/host/bright/hardware.nix</code> files. There was a little bit of back and forth over SSH from my other NixOS machine to transfer files and try to get the SSH keys set up so that the SOPS secrets configuration would work when I tried to build the Denix configuration on the new machine. I probably need to make that first bit of bootstrapping a bit smoother if I add more hosts, but it wasn’t too bad (and I made notes…). The fantastic thing with NixOS is that once you’ve got it working, any new machine automatically (if you enable the modules) gets all the features <em>and</em> configuration of your other machines. One build phase of about ten minutes and a reboot, and I had 98% of stuff set up exactly as it was on the old machine: colours, fonts, wallpapers, full editor configuration and all. It’s great!</p><p>This will sound a bit of an odd thing to praise, but I really like the BIOS on this machine. It doesn’t try to be modern-looking, or clever. It’s clean, functional, and easy to navigate, with clear help text shown next to each option. You hit <code>F1</code> to get to the BIOS options, and <code>F12</code> to go straight to boot options (i.e. to boot from a USB stick instead of the internal drive). Lovely.</p><h3>Hardware and integration</h3><p>Every hardware feature (that I’ve been able to test so far) seems to be supported in Linux and work as it should, which is amazing. There’s a fingerprint reader integrated into the power button, and once I installed the <code>fprintd</code> service, I could register fingerprints and use that to login and authenticate <code>sudo</code> requests, which is great. I got a bit more adventurous with this laptop, and set up LUKS encryption for the drive, as well as using a btrfs filesystem. I felt that full-disk encryption was probably a good idea for a machine that will leave the house.</p><p>There’s a webcam that works, with a nifty little physical privacy shutter, and even the function buttons work out of the box — even the ‘flight mode’ one, which surprised me. The integrated trackpad is delightful for swiping between windows and workspaces on Niri, though I don’t think I will ever opt to use that little joystick nubbin. Finally, I can connect it to my external screen via a Thunderbolt cable, and it works perfectly, charging while extending the internal display. There’s even a SIM card slot so I think it might be one of the models where you can use a SIM card for 4G mobile internet access. It seems as if you might be able to get this mobile modem working on Linux, but that’s an adventure for later. It might be handy when using the ThinkPad out and about if WiFi isn’t available.</p><p>Overall, everything about this laptop feels solid and reliable. It <em>feels</em> smaller and lighter than my MacBook Pro, despite the fact that they are almost exactly the same size and similar in weight. The lid opens easily with one hand, and the built-in keyboard is decently solid and positive feeling, though you still can’t pry my ZSA Voyager from my hands unless I’m travelling very light. The display is gorgeous, sharp and bright. I do wish that more manufacturers made matt displays though, as reflections can be distracting (the MacBook is the same). The only part that was in need of a refresh was the battery — no great surprise there. But I could fix that.</p><h3>Upgrades</h3><p><a href="https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/Lenovo+ThinkPad+X1+Carbon+9th+Gen+Battery+Replacement/153729">iFixit.com has a guide to battery replacement</a> on this model, and another for <a href="https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/Lenovo+ThinkPad+X1+Carbon+9th+Gen+SSD+Replacement/153733">replacing the SSD drive</a>. The first part (getting inside the back cover) is the same for both, so I decided I would do both together. It’s pretty easy to buy replacement batteries online, though it’s a good idea to be cautious about any very cheap knock-offs, which might turn out to be a lethal fire hazard. I got an OEM one that was a little more expensive, but I felt it was worth it for peace of mind.</p><p>As it happened, I already had a 1TB SSD of the right type. When I got a good deal on two 2TB NVMe drives for my desktop (obviously before the current RAMpocalypse), I moved the desktop’s original 1TB drive to an external USB enclosure and used it as a backup drive. So all I needed to do was boot the laptop from a USB stick with CloneZilla on it, clone the internal drive to the external 1TB drive, then just switch the drives over once I opened up the laptop.</p><p>I prepared myself for frustration when opening up the back of the laptop, but it all went extremely smoothly. You need only a Phillips head #1 screwdriver (no Torx or other exotic screwdrivers needed, thank you very much), and something thin and plastic to slip into the gap to gently release the clips holding the back on. Then removing the battery and the SSD cover is just a case of removing screws. I think I was in and out in under 15 minutes, with no problems at all.</p><p>I held my breath, crossed my fingers, and pressed the power button. It booted up fine first time, and I would never have known that I had switched the drives, except for having a 512GB section of free space when inspecting the drive in gparted. I couldn’t believe it went so smoothly. After that, all I needed to do was to shift the swap partition to the end of the drive, then expand the root partition to use the new space, which I did in gparted. Finally, I used <code>btrfs filesystem resize max /</code> to expand it to use the whole partition. I have a nagging feeling that I might also need to tell btrfs about tne new UUID of the drive, but I haven’t figured out how to do this yet, and it seems to boot and run fine.</p><p>I’m calibrating the new battery today, as recommended: I charged it to 100%, then used it until it automatically shut down, waited 5 hours, then charged it back up to 100% again. After that we’ll see how it goes, but it certainly took a long time to drain to zero, so I’m hopeful that it will give me a full day of usage on the battery.</p><h3>Making it my own</h3><p>I have become oddly obsessed with giving my computers appropriate host names. My desktop got called ‘morse’ which started as a lame joke relating to the first operating system I installed on it: EndeavourOS. Since I like my computers to feel that they are part of a family (or at least colleagues!) that set me off on the theme of naming them after characters in the TV series ‘Endeavour’ (of which I am a fan). I’m not sure that I can explain why, but this ThinkPad immediately presented itself as Chief Superintendent Reginald Bright, or just ‘brignt’. I think it might be because we had just re-watched all of the series again (for maybe the third time? We’ve lost count).</p><p><strong>Spoilers ahoy if you haven’t seen the very last episode of ‘Endeavour’…</strong></p><p>In one of the last scenes, we see CS Bright, now retired, sitting in the grass in a sunny location that we assume to be somewhere in India, because he has previously mentioned that he would like to return there. He is looking relaxed and happy. In contrast to his usual full ‘stiff upper lip’ style, he is wearing an outfit that the former CS Bright would probably have characterised as something that only ‘layabouts and hippies’ would wear, i.e. he has the top button of his shirt undone, and is not wearing a tie. He is also reciting Prospero’s speech from Shakespeare’s ‘The Tempest’, “Our revels now are ended…” in a dreamy way, and there’s a real sense of a man starting a new chapter of his life. Not without some sorrow, but with hope for what’s to come.</p><p>I can’t believe that I am spending so much time explaining and justifying the hostname of my computer, but to me, that sense of new beginnings struck me as being very appropriate. This little laptop has now shaken off the corporate shackles, and I will be doing the same at the end of this academic year. I’m starting to think about what my life is going to look like outside academia, and — if I can afford it — without a ‘real job’.</p><p>This also explains the stickers. I planned to put stickers on the laptop all along, but it wasn’t until I proudly held out the newly decorated CS Bright to show Mr. Bsag, that I realised that this is not a typical thing for me to do. He commented that I would never have considered doing this to one of my Macs, and he was absolutely right. It’s partly because it’s so obviously a functional thing rather than the pinnacle of engineering art that Mac hardware aspires to be. It’s also to do with having a second-hand item that already has scuffs and scratches and signs of a former life. But mostly it is me, finally taking off my metaphorical tie and undoing my metaphorical top button, and preparing myself for a new life. I’m trying to turn corporate compliance into freedom and personal expression, for both me and my new pal bright.</p><p>It’s a start, anyway.</p>